  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. Kuwait in one line: a flat 5% at the border and a port decision that changes the quote</h2>
    <p>
      “Shipping cost from China to Kuwait” is three cost groups wearing one label. <strong>Group
      one</strong> is the international move: ocean freight for FCL/LCL, or air freight by chargeable
      weight. <strong>Group two</strong> is the statutory stack — and here Kuwait is the simplest
      border in the Gulf: 5% customs duty on the CIF value, no general VAT, so the math stops at
      <code>CIF × 1.05</code>. <strong>Group three</strong> is everything after the border: THC, port
      service fees, documentation, brokerage, inspection, inland transport and the demurrage/
      detention clocks.
    </p>
    <p>
      The trap is that group two is so clean that buyers stop reading. A 5% duty makes Kuwait look
      like a solved problem — but the things that actually move a Kuwait landed cost are group one
      (fewer sailings, wider transit windows, thinner carrier competition than Dubai) and group three
      (a fee stack that is largely unpublished and a clearance process that rewards documents
      discipline). And there is a fourth variable most quotes bury in a line item: which port. Kuwait
      discharges through two very different facilities, Shuwaikh and Shuaiba, and the choice changes
      the inland leg, the terminal handling and the clearance path before a single rate is compared.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page keeps the three groups separate, prices the port decision, and computes the FCL/LCL
      crossover with Kuwait-specific numbers instead of repeating the corridor rule. If you already
      know the tax story, sections 3 and 4 are where the money actually is.
    </p>
  </section>

