  <section class="section container">
    <h2>3. Shuwaikh or Shuaiba: choose the discharge port first</h2>
    <p>
      Kuwait is one of the few Gulf markets where the port question has a real answer that changes
      the quote. <strong>Shuwaikh (KWSWK)</strong> is the country’s main commercial port — the
      gateway for containers, general cargo and consumer goods serving the capital. It sits in the
      Shuwaikh Industrial Area right beside Kuwait City, has 21 berths, and handled 613,093 TEU
      across 1,419 vessel calls in 2024. <strong>Shuaiba (KWSHB)</strong> is the industrial port, 45
      km south in Al Ahmadi: 20 berths, deeper water (roughly 10–14 m against Shuwaikh’s 6.7–10 m),
      and 282,870 TEU across 2,908 vessel calls in 2024 — more vessel calls than Shuwaikh, carrying
      less containerised box traffic, which tells you what kind of cargo it lives on.
    </p>
    <p>
      The decision logic is blunt: if your consignee and cargo are retail, consumer or general and
      bound for the capital, you want <strong>Shuwaikh</strong>. If they are industrial, chemical,
      bulk or project-heavy and tied to the southern industrial belt, you want
      <strong>Shuaiba</strong> — its deeper berths take tonnage Shuwaikh cannot. Getting this wrong
      does not just move your inland trucking cost; it changes which terminal charges apply, which
      inspection regime handles the cargo, and how long the container spends inside a port area on a
      lane where congestion is a documented constraint.
    </p>

