  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>4. The ~15 CBM question: a Kuwait-specific FCL/LCL crossover</h2>
      <p>
        The corridor’s folk rule says FCL and LCL break even near 15 CBM. That rule was built from
        the corridor’s widest LCL bracket ($20–$180/CBM) against a cheap 20ft — it is a useful
        sanity check, not a Kuwait answer. With the June 2026 Kuwait rates, we can do the actual
        arithmetic: break-even volume = FCL price ÷ LCL rate.
      </p>
      <p>
        Run it across the real bands and the answer refuses to sit still. At the top LCL rate ($75)
        against a 20ft floor ($1,500), the crossover is 20 CBM. Against a high-priced box ($2,900),
        it stretches to 38.7 CBM — more than a 20ft even holds. At the low LCL brackets, the box
        only wins beyond a 20ft’s usable volume. In other words: the crossover is not a number, it
        is a band, and the band moves with whichever pair of quotes you are actually holding.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'scenario', label: 'Scenario' },
          { key: 'arithmetic', label: 'Arithmetic' },
          { key: 'breakeven', label: 'Break-even' },
          { key: 'read', label: 'What it tells you' },
        ]}
        rows={crossoverRows}
        caption="Derived arithmetic from the June 2026 rates on this page. Break-even = FCL price ÷ LCL rate per CBM; it excludes minimum charges, CFS fees and destination differences, so treat it as the start of the decision, not the end."
      />
      <SourceList sources={rateSources} title="Sources — FCL vs LCL crossover" />

      <div class="grid grid--2 mt-2">
        <Card title="The three multipliers that matter">
          <p>
            Your crossover moves with three things: the LCL rate you were quoted, the FCL price you
            were quoted, and your true usable volume. Change any one and the breakpoint moves.
            Recompute with your quote pair — not with this page’s midpoints.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card title="The minimum-charge trap">
          <p>
            Small LCL consignments often hit a minimum charge that makes the effective per-CBM rate
            far higher than the headline. Ask for the minimum, then re-run the break-even with the
            effective number.
          </p>
        </Card>
      </div>

      <div class="note mt-2">
        <strong>Kuwait-specific verdict:</strong> the corridor rule (~15 CBM) is the optimistic
        edge of the band; Kuwait’s own rates put the practical 20ft crossover at roughly 20–39 CBM.
        If you ship 18 CBM, the right answer is “request both quotes” — you are close enough to the
        line that the economics flip on whichever provider is hungrier that month.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>5. Kuwait’s 24-day clock: typical, range, and the schedule-density tax</h2>
    <p>
      Route data gives Shuwaikh a typical <strong>24 days</strong> with a 15–30 day range, from all
      three main China origins (LOW confidence — it is an estimate). The June 2026 carrier sheet
      goes further in both directions: 22–38 days from Shanghai and 25–45 days for LCL. That gap
      between the route-data ceiling (30) and the carrier-sheet ceiling (38) is the schedule-density
      tax in numbers — Kuwait is a lower-frequency lane, most services transship via Singapore or a
      regional hub, and the slow end of the range does not care that the average looks fine.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do the buffer math before you book: a 24-day plan against a 30-day window carries 6 extra days
      of stock; against a 38-day carrier promise it carries 14. On consumer goods with storage costs
      and on project cargo with installation dates, that buffer is the real cost of this lane — more
      than the 5% duty will ever be. Two more clock effects to hold: Gulf-side routing has spent
      2026 absorbing geopolitical premia and routing changes, and any transshipment adds a handling
      point where your container joins someone else’s schedule.
    </p>

