  <section class="section container">
    <h2>7. The $10,000 CIF walkthrough at Kuwait’s border</h2>
    <p>
      Build the CIF value from cost, insurance and freight; apply the 5% duty; stop. With no
      general VAT in force, the Kuwait statutory stack is the simplest in the GCC —
      <code>CIF × 1.05</code> and nothing re-taxes the duty.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'layer', label: 'Cost layer' },
        { key: 'calc', label: 'How to obtain it' },
        { key: 'amount', label: 'Indicative amount' },
        { key: 'note', label: 'Planning note' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={cifRows}
      caption="CIF $10,000 is an illustrative round figure only. Replace it with your actual declared value, freight and insurance."
    />
    <SourceList sources={taxSources} title="Sources — CIF and landed-cost layers" />

    <h3 class="mt-3">The worked example, end to end</h3>
    <p>
      For a $10,000 CIF shipment, the 5% duty is $500 and the statutory landed cost is
      <strong>$10,500</strong>. Compare that across the region: the same CIF clears at $11,025 in
      the UAE or Oman (×1.1025), $11,550 in Bahrain (×1.155) and $12,075 in Saudi Arabia
      (×1.2075). Kuwait’s border advantage is $1,575 against Saudi Arabia, $1,050 against Bahrain
      and $525 against the UAE — a real, structural gap that survives rate volatility. Now note
      what that gap is worth against the rest of the bill: the freight band on this lane spans
      $1,400 per box between its low and high prints, and the transit spread runs from 22 to 38
      days. The tax saving is the smallest moving part of a Kuwait landed cost — which is why the
      rest of this page spends its time on freight, ports and time.
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid--2 mt-2">
      <Card title="The formula in one line">
        <p class="lead">
          <strong>CIF × (1 + duty rate)</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Baseline: CIF × 1.05 — the border stops at duty. Add destination fees on top and keep
          the VAT question on a watch list rather than in the budget.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="What would change this page">
        <p>
          Kuwait has signed the GCC VAT framework but not implemented VAT — no law as of April
          2026, and no expectation before 2028. The framework itself was amended in May 2026,
          so this is a number to re-check annually, not to assume forever.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>

    <div class="note mt-2">
      <strong>Under-declaration is not a saving:</strong> the CIF value must reflect cost,
      insurance and freight. A low invoice invites revaluation, penalties and delay — and on a
      lane with fewer sailings and a document-heavy clearance, delay is the most expensive line
      item you can create for yourself.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>8. When air wins on a no-VAT lane</h2>
      <p>
        Kuwait’s low tax burden makes one thing easy to get backwards: air freight does not get
        cheaper because there is no VAT. Duty applies to air cargo the same way it applies to sea —
        5% of CIF — so air must win on time and money, not on tax. The corridor’s indicative air band
        sits at $4.6–$8.2/kg, which prices air as a tool for urgency, launches and value-dense cargo,
        not as a volume solution. For a 200 kg consignment you are looking at roughly $920–$1,640 of
        air freight before destination fees — comparable to a discounted 20ft on some weeks, and
        that comparison is exactly why the decision should be made with two quotes, not one instinct.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two mechanics decide the air bill before the rate does. First, <strong>chargeable
        weight</strong>: air cargo is billed on the greater of actual and volumetric weight, so a box
        of light, bulky goods can weigh 40 kg on the scale and bill as 60 kg or more. Second,
        <strong>what is bundled</strong>: DDP air quotes fold clearance and duty into one number,
        which is convenient but hard to audit. If you self-clear sea cargo regularly, price the air
        leg the same way and compare the all-in totals.
      </p>

