    />
    <SourceList sources={costSources} title="Sources — Kuwait cost stack" />

    <h3 class="mt-3">Demurrage and detention</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Demurrage</strong> is the terminal’s charge when a container overstays inside the
      port after discharge; <strong>detention</strong> is the carrier’s charge when the container
      overstays outside the port after collection. Free-time allowances and per-day rates differ
      by line and terminal and were not published — so the booking confirmation is your only
      evidence when a clock starts. On a lower-frequency lane the stakes run higher than usual:
      if your container misses its slot in the discharge and trucking sequence, the next
      opportunity may be days away at Shuwaikh’s urban terminal, where congestion is a known
      constraint.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-3">What actually moves a Kuwait bill</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Schedule density</strong> — fewer sailings mean wider windows; a slow-end transit is inventory you finance, not a rate you negotiate.</li>
      <li><strong>Discharge port choice</strong> — Shuwaikh or Shuaiba changes the inland leg, the terminal handling and the clearance path.</li>
      <li><strong>LCL minimums and CFS handling</strong> — small consignments can carry an effective per-CBM rate well above the headline band.</li>
      <li><strong>Documentation and PAI certification timing</strong> — late paperwork delays clearance and starts free-time clocks.</li>
      <li><strong>Free-time discipline</strong> — the cheapest demurrage is the demurrage you never incur; pre-clear so the box moves.</li>
      <li><strong>Surcharge schedule</strong> — BAF/CAF and peak-season lines move the total faster than base rates; ask for them in writing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="mt-3">Cost discipline on the no-VAT lane</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Consolidate past your crossover so you are buying container space, not top-rate LCL space.</li>
      <li>Quote both discharge ports when the consignee’s location allows it — then compare all-in.</li>
      <li>Classify the HS code and screen restricted goods before booking.</li>
      <li>Start PAI certification in the booking stage for regulated goods.</li>
      <li>File the import declaration before the vessel arrives.</li>
      <li>Book trucking before discharge to protect free time.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>10. Documents, PAI certification and the clearance order</h2>

      <h3>Tax and duty — the short version</h3>
      <p>
        Kuwait’s baseline import duty is <strong>5% of CIF value</strong> under the GCC Common
        External Tariff, and no general VAT is in force, so the statutory landed cost for most goods
        is <code>CIF × 1.05</code>. Food and some pharmaceuticals can be duty-free; alcohol and
        tobacco run higher lines. The HS code is still the first cost decision — and the first thing
        a broker needs to see.
      </p>

      <h3>Kuwait clearance order</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Classify the goods with the correct <strong>HS code</strong> before quoting or booking.</li>
        <li>Confirm importer registration and any PAI (Public Authority for Industry) certification for regulated products.</li>
        <li>Prepare the commercial invoice, packing list, certificate of origin and bill of lading or air waybill.</li>
        <li>File the import declaration through the Kuwait Customs electronic system — ideally before the vessel arrives.</li>
        <li>Pay the assessed 5% duty, pass inspection if selected, and release the cargo.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Documents that decide the week</h3>
      <p>
        Standard documents are the commercial invoice, bill of lading or air waybill, packing list,
        certificate of origin and any permits for restricted goods; add PAI certification where it
        applies. <strong>ISF</strong> is a United States requirement and does not apply to Kuwait —
        Kuwait’s filing step is the import declaration. Because clearance here is document-heavy,
        the practical rule is to have every document correct before the vessel sails, not before it
        arrives. For a deeper walkthrough, see the
        <a href="/middleeast/kuwait-customs-duty/">Kuwait customs and duty page</a> and the
        <a href="/middleeast/shipping-from-china-to-kuwait/">main China-to-Kuwait guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>No SABER, no SASO — but not no rules</h3>
      <p>
        SABER and SASO are Saudi-only conformity systems; do not copy them into a Kuwait import.
        Kuwait’s own regime leans on Customs documentation and PAI certification for certain
        products, and while the exact scope depends on your HS code, the lesson is the same as
        everywhere in the Gulf: conformity screens are commodity-specific and belong in the quoting
        stage. For how Kuwait’s duty-and-VAT position compares across all six GCC states, the
        <a href="/middleeast/gcc-customs-duty-comparison/">GCC customs and duty comparison</a> lays
        the multipliers side by side.
      </p>

      <SourceList sources={taxSources} title="Sources — Kuwait customs, tax & clearance" />
    </div>
