  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>The border is the easy part: ×1.05 means $10,000 CIF clears at $10,500 — $1,575 under Saudi Arabia’s ×1.2075, with no VAT layer to re-tax the duty. Nothing on the tax side needs a negotiation.</li>
      <li>The June 2026 rate sheet gives you real bands — $1,500–$2,900 per 20ft, $2,200–$4,400 per 40ft, $35–$75/CBM for LCL — and both China origins price within a whisker of each other, so pick on service, not geography.</li>
      <li>The ~15 CBM crossover is the optimistic edge of a band: on Kuwait’s own rates a 20ft breaks even against top-rate LCL at ~20 CBM and not until ~39 CBM against a high-priced box. Quote both ways anywhere near the line.</li>
      <li>The clock is the cost: a 24-day typical sits inside windows that carrier sheets stretch to 38 days, so build 6–14 days of buffer and treat schedule reliability as the thing worth paying for.</li>
      <li>Pick the port before the price: Shuwaikh (613,093 TEU in 2024, beside the capital) for consumer and general cargo, Shuaiba (282,870 TEU, deeper berths, 45 km south) for industrial and project freight — a quote that does not name its port is not finished.</li>
    </ol>
