  <section class="section container">
    <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
    <p><strong>Position:</strong> In my view, Kuwait is the rare lane where the tax code is the easy part — I would book the ×1.05 border without a second thought and spend the entire negotiation on schedule density and the discharge port, because that is where a $1,575 tax saving quietly disappears.</p>
    <ul><li>×1.05 lands $10,000 CIF at $10,500 — $1,575 under Saudi Arabia — and nothing re-taxes the duty; the border math is settled and knowable, which is more than almost any other number on this lane.</li><li>The June 2026 sheets span 22–38 days from Shanghai against a 24-day typical, and every slow-end day is inventory you finance; schedule reliability is the real purchase decision here.</li><li>The FCL/LCL break-even band runs roughly 20–39 CBM on Kuwait’s own rates; an 18 CBM shipment should be quoted both ways before anyone declares a winner — the ~15 CBM corridor rule is the optimistic edge, not the answer.</li></ul>
    <p class="note"><em>Disclosure: This is the author's editorial judgment based on the published data on this page — not an independently verified fact.</em></p>
